Z||000002||Shiner||11-01-1999||04:25 PM||||I JUST replaced the relay, and the fan DOES work, I checked that.  What do you think now? The one wire going to the temp switch was hot too.  I just dont know.  Help me, oh great ogre!||||reg||9
Z||000003||theogre||11-01-1999||05:50 PM||||how did you check the fan? inplace or did you pull it out? If you pulled it out, you might still have a bad ground up front.<P>You are sure your at the right sensor. Have someone watch the dash as you ground the green lines. If you are on the wrong one either the temp light will come on, or the temp gauge will peg. ((All 3 circuits activate by grounding the line. the temp gauge grounds thru a thermistor. All 3 use green wires. It can be hard to tell them apart when they are old.))<P>One of the green lines lines should activate the fan. <P>check the fan fuse under the dash. This fuse only controls the fan relay's green side. The heavy current side of the fan wiring is on a fuse link in the back.<P>If you ground the line at the sensor and the fan comes on, but still won't spin with the AC then likely the AC control is flaky.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-01-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eddie||11-01-1999||06:32 PM||||help	<BR>I have ? for all of you as will about my fan please help me if you can. Ok if I ground the green/wht wire up at the relay the fan will come on. It also comes on with the AC.But if I ground it out at the temp switch (thats the one in the upper left hand corner right ?) IT WILL NOT turn ON the temp light comes at 235 240 so I HAVE BEEN just turning  on the AC to  cool it off . But I WOULD LIKE TO FIX IT the right way. PS IF I GROUND IT  OUT AT THE SWITCH IT PEGS AM I AT THE RIGHT SWITCH YES NO WHAT PLEASE HELP EDDIE||||reg||8
Z||000005||Shiner||11-01-1999||09:18 PM||||Ogre,<P> I checked the fan in place by disconnecting the lines, and running 12v DC to it.  Fired right up.  The red line to the relay IS getting power.  The temp switch is that three pronged thing near the water pump, correct?  The A/C does not actually have a belt on it, I suspect that the bearings froze or something, and they just pulled the belt.||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||11-02-1999||01:03 PM||||From what I've seen the AC control should activate the fan wether or not the compressor runs. The book seems to make out that the AC uses it's own relay to bypass the sender to ground.<P>the 2 senders by the thermostat are for the light gauge and ECM. I think your ok there.<P>try jumping the red leads on the relay connector. if you have a bad ground to the fan that should tell you real quick.||||reg||1
